    "presentation_long_en": "On Tam Hai Island in the Nui Thanh area, at the southern tip of Da Nang City (formerly Quang Nam Province), the Ban Than Rocks are a striking and still-unspoilt natural wonder. They form a natural band of black stone stretching hundreds of metres along the shore, made up of countless boulders in strange shapes, their surfaces glossy and dark, layered and fractured by geological processes over millions of years and the ceaseless wear of wind and waves. Scientists regard the rock formation here as of special geological value, recording the ancient history of the land. Around the rocks lie clear blue seas, sandy beaches and a coral reef close to shore, creating a scene both grand and poetic that greatly appeals to travellers who love exploring and photography. Tam Hai is an islet ringed by river and sea; to reach it visitors usually take a ferry, then explore the Ban Than rocks and cape, Nom beach and the peaceful fishing-village life. With few tourist facilities as yet, it keeps a pristine character well suited to day trips for immersing oneself in the island-and-sea nature of central Vietnam.",
